#!/usr/bin/env python3
# unit-registry: a greppable code registry for the FreeUnit C tree.
#
# It answers, from flat text artifacts and in milliseconds, the questions that
# otherwise cost minutes of ad-hoc `rg` and are easy to get wrong:
#
#   who calls this           unit-registry callers nxt_utf8_encode
#   is it dead in production unit-registry uses nxt_utf8_is_valid
#   what does the binary     unit-registry abi
#   export that nothing uses
#   where is this constant   unit-registry const NXT_HTTP_REQUEST_TIMEOUT
#   what includes what       unit-registry includes src/nxt_conf.c
#
# (These five answer on FreeUnit master at d54b109a.)
#
# There is no database. The index is a directory of TSV files, greppable with
# the same `rg` you were already reaching for, diffable between two refs, and
# reviewable by eye. `unit-registry index` rebuilds it in a few seconds
# (4.5 to 5 s for the FreeUnit tree on a laptop). The cross-check needs ast-grep on PATH or
# --astgrep; without it `index` fails, and --no-crosscheck builds an index
# that `verify` refuses as unverified.
#
# The file:line citations and counts in this header and in the comments below
# are the cases that shaped the tool, as they stood on the tree each was found
# on. Lines drift and some symbols are gone (nxt_utf8_casecmp, nxt_utf8_next,
# nxt_fiber_main_create are not in FreeUnit master): read them as history.
# `verify` pins the ones that still matter, and skips a check whose symbol is
# absent at the indexed ref.
#
#
# WHY THIS EXTRACTION STRATEGY
#
# Unit inherits nginx's house style, and one rule of it is mechanically
# load-bearing here: a function *definition* puts the return type on its own
# line, so the function name starts at column 1 (docs/style.md, verified
# against the tree). That makes the top level of a translation unit cheap to
# scan exactly, without a C parser.
#
# So the primary extractor is a two-stage lexer:
#
#   1. a character state machine that blanks comments, string and character
#      literals, and preprocessor directives, preserving offsets;
#   2. a brace-depth scanner over the blanked text that walks the top level of
#      the translation unit and classifies each statement.
#
# Stage 1 is not an optimisation, it is the correctness step. Without it
#   nxt_log_alert(thr->log, "nxt_utf8_casecmp() failed")
# registers as a call to nxt_utf8_casecmp (src/test/nxt_utf8_test.c:185) and
#   /* nxt_utf8_next() expects a valid UTF-8 string. */
# registers as a reference to a function that in fact has none anywhere in the
# tree (src/nxt_utf8.h:29). Both were real lines in the tree this was written
# on; a registry that counts them is worse than no registry, because it is
# confidently wrong.
#
# Alternatives considered and rejected:
#
#   tree-sitter: this is the tool that already failed here. A previous index
#     silently truncated nxt_router.c to 44% of its lines, because parse ERROR
#     nodes ended extraction early and the chunk count still looked plausible.
#     A whole-file lexer cannot truncate: it either reaches EOF or raises.
#   clang / compile_commands.json: needs a configured build, so it cannot
#     index a branch, and the registry must index the main branch and an
#     open branch without building either.
#   universal-ctags: it is not a harness dependency, and it does not carry
#     the production-vs-test classification that is the point of the usage
#     registry.
#   ast-grep alone: it *is* a real C parser and it is fast, but it fails to an
#     empty result set rather than an error, so a broken query and a clean file
#     are indistinguishable. It is used here as an independent cross-check
#     (below) rather than as the source of truth.
#
#
# THE GATES
#
# Each of these exists because of a specific way this has already gone wrong.
# They run on every `index` and a failure is loud and non-zero-exit.
#
#   coverage   For every indexed file the scanner accounts for each line as
#              function body, top-level declaration, object, directive, comment
#              or blank, and compares the total against wc -l. A non-trivial .c
#              file yielding zero function definitions is a hard failure. Never
#              trust a symbol count on its own: the truncated index had a
#              plausible one.
#   balance    A file that ends at nonzero brace depth, a body that runs to EOF
#              without closing, or a `}` seen at depth 0, all mean a definition
#              has swallowed the rest of the translation unit. Coverage alone
#              cannot catch this: a runaway body "accounts for" every line it
#              eats, which is exactly how nxt_openssl.c read as fully covered
#              while 23 of its 27 definitions were missing. A stray `}` must
#              not be clamped to zero either. That let a mutated nxt_router.c
#              lose 105 of 106 definitions and still report depth 0.
#   crosscheck ast-grep re-extracts function definitions with a real C grammar.
#              Per-file name-set disagreements are recorded as findings, and a
#              shortfall (ast-grep finding 4+ definitions where the scanner
#              found under half) FAILS the run. Merely recording it is not
#              enough: a mutated nxt_router.c that kept 1 of its 106
#              definitions reported "1 vs 106" here and still exited 0. An
#              empty ast-grep result tree-wide, or a nonzero exit, is treated
#              as a broken cross-check, never as agreement. Where an #if/#else
#              straddles a brace the scanner reads each other branch in a
#              second pass, so a definition only that branch opens is still
#              indexed; one ast-grep finds there and no pass does is reported
#              (disagreements.tsv, MANIFEST conditional_hidden_files), not
#              fatal.
#   read       A source file or directory that cannot be read, an unreadable
#              auto/make, auto/sources or auto/modules script, and a .c file
#              that reads as C++, fail the index: its references, the
#              classification or the public ABI would be missing.
#   column1    The house style predicts the definition name sits at column 1.
#              Where the scanner and that prediction disagree the definition is
#              flagged. The outliers in the tree (MANIFEST col1_outliers) are
#              genuine style outliers (the return type sharing the name's line,
#              as for nxt_conf_vldt_match_encoded_patterns_sets in
#              src/nxt_conf_validation.c), not parser confusion.
#
# The git ref indexed is stamped in MANIFEST, and --ref indexes any ref without
# touching your working tree. That is load-bearing rather than tidy:
# nxt_utf8_is_valid once had zero callers on the main branch and one production
# caller on an open fix branch. A registry that only knew the main branch would
# have reported it dead and got it deleted.
#
# NAME RESOLUTION IS BY BARE NAME, WITH ONE EXCEPTION
#
# A reference is recorded against the identifier it spells, not against a
# resolved declaration, so two functions that share a name share their
# references in `uses` and `callers`. Parameters, block-scope locals and
# struct or union member declarations are the exception the other way: in
# its own scope a local is the local, so it is never recorded as a use of a
# file-scope symbol of the same name. The one place a symbol is scoped is `dead`:
# a `static` definition in a .c file counts only the references in its own
# file, and a reference in a file that has its own static of that name is not
# credited to any other definition. Statics in headers (`nxt_inline`) are
# still matched by bare name, because every includer may use them. Treat
# `dead` as a candidate list to confirm by reading, never as proof.
#
# WHAT IT DOES NOT SEE
#
#   Struct and union members, parameters and locals are not symbols: only
#   file-scope names are registered. A local that shares a file-scope name
#   (`error`, `handler`) is not counted as a use of it inside the local's
#   scope (the rest of its block; a `for` declaration, that loop). A local
#   declared by a macro, or inside a GNU statement expression, is not seen.
#   A whole-body macro, one whose expansion holds a function's braces, hides
#   that function from the scanner (the directive is blanked) and from
#   ast-grep alike, with every gate green. A function defined through a
#   macro invocation, PHP_FUNCTION(name) { ... }, is registered under its
#   argument; the macro row (form=macro) is not a function and `dead` skips
#   it. Calls through function pointers and token pasting are not seen. Go
#   is read only for `C.name` (see SCAN_ROOTS). A K&R (old-style) definition,
#   `int f(a, b) int a; int b; { ... }`, is not indexed, and its parameter
#   declarations read as file-scope objects; FreeUnit has none. Generated
#   headers (build/include/nxt_auto_config.h, nxt_version.h) are not read, so
#   NXT_VERSION and the NXT_HAVE_* values of configure are unknown names.
#   Known limits, none of which the FreeUnit tree hits today: a definition
#   that returns a function pointer shows a cut signature; a parameter is
#   excluded by line, so a body use on the line of a parameter declaration
#   is dropped; a block-scope `extern int y;` reads as a new local, so its
#   uses in that block are not counted for the file-scope `y`.
#
# A constant (a #define or an enum member) named in #if, #elif, #ifdef,
# #ifndef or defined() is a use of kind `pp`. It is counted in `uses`, `const`
# and `dead`, and it is neither a call nor an address-taken reference, so
# `callers` does not list it. Any other name there is not a use: the
# preprocessor knows no functions or objects. The #ifndef of an include guard
# (the first conditional of a file, followed at once by `#define` of the same
# name) is not a use: an include guard reports 0 uses unless some other file
# tests it. Only that first form is a guard: a guard after an `extern "C"`
# block, or a second one in the same file, counts as a `pp` use.
#
# An #if branch is dropped when its condition is constant false. pp_const()
# reads decimal and octal integers, defined(), `! + - * < > <= >= == != && ||`
# and parentheses. Anything else, such as a hex literal, an unsigned literal,
# `?:` or a macro name, makes the condition unknown and the branch is kept.
#
# `callers` lists direct calls, then a separate "address-taken (not a direct
# call):" group: every non-call reference, such as `rp->handler = &fn;`. The
# dispatch through that pointer is not seen, so `--transitive` walks call
# edges only and ends with the count of address-taken references it did not
# follow.
#
# Reads the source tree; never writes to it. An index whose gates failed still
# writes its artifacts, so they can be inspected, but every query except
# `stat` refuses to answer from it. A source file or directory that cannot be
# read is a gate failure: its references would otherwise vanish silently. `verify` also
# refuses an index built with --no-crosscheck or missing an artifact.

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import json
import os
import re
import shutil
import subprocess
import sys
